      content: 'A 68-year-old right-handed retired firefighter presents to the PM&R clinic with a 2-year history of progressive bilateral leg pain and heaviness that worsens with walking and prolonged standing. He describes the pain as a deep aching and burning sensation in both buttocks, thighs, and calves that begins after walking approximately 1-2 blocks and forces him to stop and sit down or lean forward on a shopping cart for relief. He notes that walking uphill is easier than walking downhill and that riding a stationary bicycle is painless. He has tried over-the-counter NSAIDs with minimal relief. He also reports mild low back pain but emphasizes that the leg symptoms are far more bothersome. His PCP ordered an MRI showing moderate-to-severe central canal stenosis at L3-4 and L4-5 with ligamentum flavum hypertrophy and facet arthropathy. He is a Jehovah\'s Witness and states upfront: "My faith does not allow blood transfusions, and I need you to respect that no matter what treatment we discuss." He is accompanied by his wife.',
