    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Claudication characterization**: Onset distance (how far can he walk before symptoms start), maximum walking distance, time to symptom relief with sitting (neurogenic claudication typically resolves within minutes of sitting or flexion). Positional relief pattern — does leaning on a shopping cart (flexed posture) extend walking distance? This is the classic "shopping cart sign."\n- **Neurogenic vs vascular claudication**: Critical differential. Ask about peripheral vascular disease risk factors (smoking history as a firefighter, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia). Palpate peripheral pulses. Key distinguishing features:\n  - Neurogenic: Relief with flexion (sitting, leaning forward), worse walking downhill (extension), bicycle riding painless, pulses present\n  - Vascular: Relief with standing still (does not require sitting), worse walking uphill, bicycle riding also painful, pulses diminished\n- **Red flag symptoms**: Saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction (cauda equina syndrome), progressive neurologic deficit, fever, unexplained weight loss, history of cancer.\n- **Back pain component**: Mechanical vs inflammatory pattern. Worse with extension (stenosis) vs flexion (disc). Morning stiffness duration.\n- **Neurologic symptoms**: Numbness, tingling, weakness in specific dermatomal/myotomal patterns. Foot drop. Balance changes.\n- **Functional impact**: Walking tolerance, stairs, community mobility, recreational activities, sleep disruption. Swiss Spinal Stenosis Questionnaire.\n- **Prior treatments**: PT (what type — flexion-based vs extension-based matters), injections, chiropractic, medications.\n- **Surgical history**: Any prior lumbar surgery. This affects surgical planning if surgery becomes indicated.\n- **Religious beliefs and treatment boundaries**: Jehovah\'s Witness faith — blood transfusion refusal is a sincere, protected religious belief. This has important implications for surgical planning and informed consent.',
