      content: '**Vitals**: BP 142/86 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, Weight 198 lbs, BMI 28.5.\n\n**General**: Well-developed male, walks into clinic with mildly antalgic gait. No acute distress at rest.\n\n**Lumbar Spine**:\n- Inspection: Mild loss of lumbar lordosis. No scoliosis.\n- ROM: Extension limited to 10 degrees and reproduces bilateral buttock and posterior thigh pain. Flexion 60 degrees with mild LBP only. Lateral bending mildly limited bilaterally.\n- Palpation: Tenderness over L3-5 spinous processes and bilateral paraspinal muscles. Facet loading sign positive bilaterally.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: Bilateral hip flexion 5/5, knee extension 5/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5 bilaterally (subtle weakness), EHL 4+/5 bilaterally, ankle plantarflexion 5/5. Single heel raise: can perform bilaterally but fatigues after 5 repetitions (abnormal for age).\n- **Sensory**: Mildly reduced light touch sensation in bilateral L5 dermatomes (dorsal foot). Intact L4 and S1 dermatomes.\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar 2+ bilaterally, Achilles 1+ bilaterally (diminished). No pathologic reflexes. Babinski absent bilaterally.\n- **Straight Leg Raise**: Negative bilaterally (SLR tests nerve root tension from disc herniation — expected to be negative in stenosis).\n- **Provocative testing**: **Stoop test**: Patient walks until symptoms reproduce (approximately 3 minutes of walking in the hallway), then flexes forward — symptoms improve within 30 seconds. This is pathognomonic for neurogenic claudication.\n\n**Vascular Exam**: Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses 2+ bilaterally. No femoral bruits. Ankle-brachial index (ABI) 1.1 bilaterally (normal >0.9). Skin warm, no trophic changes. Hair distribution normal.\n\n**Hip Exam**: Full ROM bilaterally. No groin pain with flexion and internal rotation (FADIR negative). Hip pathology excluded as a pain generator.\n\n**MRI Findings (reviewed)**: Moderate-to-severe central canal stenosis at L3-4 and L4-5 secondary to ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, facet arthropathy with synovial cysts, and mild disc bulging. Thecal sac AP diameter <10 mm at both levels (moderate stenosis 10-12 mm, severe <10 mm). No disc herniation. Mild foraminal stenosis bilaterally at L4-5. Conus terminates at L1 level (normal).',
