    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the pathophysiology of neurogenic claudication, when EMG is indicated, and the complete differential diagnosis.',
      content: '**Pathophysiology of Neurogenic Claudication**:\n\nNeurogenic claudication results from dynamic compression of the cauda equina nerve roots within a narrowed spinal canal. The mechanism involves:\n\n1. **Positional stenosis**: Lumbar extension narrows the central canal by 9-15% (thickening of ligamentum flavum, facet override, disc bulging posteriorly). Flexion opens the canal. This is why walking downhill (extension) is worse and cycling (flexion) is painless.\n2. **Venous congestion hypothesis**: Canal stenosis compresses epidural veins, causing venous congestion and impaired arterial inflow to nerve roots. With walking (which increases neural metabolic demand), the nerve roots become ischemic. This explains the exercise-induced symptom onset and rest-related relief.\n3. **Mechanical compression**: Direct mechanical compression of nerve roots causes demyelination and axonal injury, producing the neurologic deficits (weakness, numbness).\n4. **Inflammatory component**: Stenosis creates a local inflammatory environment with elevated levels of IL-6, TNF-alpha, and substance P around compressed nerve roots.\n\n**Role of EMG/NCS in Lumbar Stenosis**:\n\nEMG is NOT required for diagnosis when clinical presentation and MRI are concordant. However, EMG is indicated when:\n1. **Clinical-radiographic mismatch**: Symptoms do not correlate with MRI findings (e.g., MRI shows mild stenosis but symptoms are severe, or MRI shows multilevel stenosis but clinical picture suggests a specific level)\n2. **Peripheral neuropathy coexists**: Common in this age group — diabetic neuropathy or other polyneuropathies can mimic or coexist with stenosis. EMG helps distinguish radiculopathy from neuropathy.\n3. **Surgical planning**: To identify which levels are clinically significant when multilevel stenosis is present\n4. **Baseline documentation**: Before intervention, to measure against post-treatment results\n\n**Expected EMG findings in lumbar stenosis**:\n- Needle EMG: Denervation (fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) in a multi-root distribution (L3-S1 myotomes) — polyradiculopathy pattern\n- Paraspinal fibrillations are common\n- Nerve conduction studies: May show reduced CMAP amplitudes in affected motor nerves (peroneal, tibial). H-reflex may be prolonged or absent bilaterally. **Normal sensory NCS helps distinguish from peripheral neuropathy (where SNAPs would be reduced).**\n\n**Complete Differential Diagnosis of Leg Pain with Walking**:\n\n1. **Vascular claudication**: Peripheral arterial disease. Key differences from neurogenic: pain with uphill walking (not downhill), relief with standing still (does not require sitting/flexion), worsened by cycling, diminished pulses, abnormal ABI (<0.9). This patient\'s normal ABI and pulses exclude vascular claudication.\n2. **Hip osteoarthritis**: Groin pain with walking, limited ROM (especially internal rotation), pain reproduced by FADIR test. Excluded by normal hip exam.\n3. **Trochanteric bursitis / gluteal tendinopathy**: Lateral hip pain, worse with side-lying, positive lateral hip tenderness.\n4. **Peripheral neuropathy**: Symmetric stocking-glove sensory loss, burning/tingling, not positional, not relieved by flexion. EMG/NCS distinguishes.\n5. **Chronic exertional compartment syndrome**: Younger patients, exercise-induced anterior leg tightness and pain, resolves with rest, compartment pressure testing diagnostic.\n6. **Lumbar disc herniation**: Usually unilateral radicular pain in a single dermatome, positive SLR, typically younger patients. MRI shows disc herniation rather than canal stenosis.\n7. **Spondylolisthesis**: Can cause stenosis (degenerative spondylolisthesis, especially L4-5). Standing lateral X-ray assesses stability.\n8. **Cauda equina syndrome**: Acute emergency — saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, bilateral leg weakness. Must be excluded if acute neurologic changes occur.',
