      title: '+ Outline the conservative treatment pathway, epidural injection approach, and criteria for surgical referral.',
      content: '**Conservative Management — First-Line Approach**:\n\n**Physical Therapy (Flexion-Based Program)**:\n- **Flexion-based exercises are the cornerstone**: Williams flexion exercises, posterior pelvic tilts, knee-to-chest stretches, lumbar flexion mobilizations. These open the spinal canal and reduce nerve root compression.\n- **Avoid extension-based programs**: McKenzie extension exercises (used for disc herniation) will WORSEN stenosis symptoms.\n- **Aerobic conditioning**: Stationary bicycle (flexion-based, painless for this patient), aquatic therapy (buoyancy offloads spine, water resistance provides conditioning), recumbent elliptical\n- **Core stabilization**: Abdominal bracing, transversus abdominis activation, multifidus training — in a neutral-to-flexion bias\n- **Gait training**: Walking with a rollator walker with forearm supports maintains slight lumbar flexion during ambulation and may extend walking distance\n- **Neural mobilization**: Gentle nerve gliding exercises for the lower extremities\n- **Duration**: 6-12 weeks of supervised PT before reassessment\n\n**Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Trial of prescription-strength (meloxicam 15 mg daily or naproxen 500 mg BID) — he has only tried OTC doses. GI protection with PPI given his age.\n- **Gabapentin or pregabalin**: For neuropathic component (burning, tingling). Start gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime, titrate to 300 mg TID or higher as tolerated. Evidence is mixed for stenosis but reasonable for neuropathic symptoms.\n- **Calcitonin**: Limited evidence for neurogenic claudication. Some studies show modest benefit. Can be considered as adjunct.\n- **Acetaminophen**: Adjunctive for mild pain. Avoid opioids.\n- **Muscle relaxants**: Short-term if significant paraspinal spasm contributes.\n\n**Epidural Steroid Injections (ESIs)**:\n- **Approach**: Interlaminar or transforaminal epidural injection at L3-4 and/or L4-5 under fluoroscopic guidance\n- **For central stenosis**: Interlaminar approach delivers medication to the central canal where the compression is located\n- **Evidence**: Moderate evidence for short-to-intermediate-term pain relief (weeks to months) in lumbar stenosis. Does NOT change the natural history of stenosis. Role is primarily as a bridge — to improve function enough for effective PT participation, or to temporize while deciding on surgery.\n- **Protocol**: Reasonable to try a series of up to 3 injections over 6-12 months. If no benefit after 2 injections, additional injections are unlikely to help.\n- **Risks**: Infection, bleeding, dural puncture, transient worsening, rare neurologic injury. In this patient who refuses blood transfusion, the extremely low risk of epidural hematoma should be discussed and documented, though the risk is negligible with standard technique.\n\n**Surgical Referral Criteria**:\n1. **Failed adequate conservative treatment** (minimum 3-6 months of PT, medications, and/or injections)\n2. **Progressive neurologic deficit** (worsening weakness, foot drop, cauda equina symptoms — these are urgent/emergent indications regardless of conservative trial)\n3. **Intolerable functional limitation** despite conservative care — the patient defines what is intolerable\n4. **Concordance between imaging and clinical presentation** — the surgical level must match the clinical syndrome\n\n**Surgical Options**:\n- **Laminectomy/decompression**: Standard posterior decompression. Removes lamina and ligamentum flavum to open the canal. Most common procedure for stenosis. No fusion required unless instability is present.\n- **Laminotomy**: Less invasive, preserves more bony architecture. Bilateral decompression through a unilateral approach.\n- **Interspinous process device (X-STOP)**: Limits extension and maintains canal opening. Less invasive but higher reoperation rate. Limited by insurance coverage.\n- **Laminectomy with fusion**: Required if spondylolisthesis, scoliosis, or iatrogenic instability from wide decompression.\n\n**SPORT Trial Evidence**: The Spine Patient Outcomes Research Trial showed that surgical decompression provided significantly greater improvement than conservative care for lumbar stenosis at 2 and 4 years, but the conservative group also improved, and crossover between groups was high. Shared decision-making is essential.',
