    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the shared decision-making framework for surgical vs conservative treatment, incorporating the patient\'s values and religious beliefs.',
      content: '**Shared Decision-Making Framework for Lumbar Stenosis Surgery**:\n\nLumbar spinal stenosis is a condition where surgery is elective (absent progressive neurologic deficit or cauda equina syndrome). This makes it an ideal candidate for shared decision-making — there is no single "right" answer, and the patient\'s values, preferences, and life circumstances should heavily influence the decision.\n\n**Step 1 — Present the Options Clearly**:\n\'There are two reasonable paths forward:\n\n**Path 1 — Continued conservative care**: Physical therapy focused on flexion exercises, medications, and possibly epidural injections. The evidence shows that many patients with stenosis improve or stabilize with conservative treatment, though it may not fully resolve your symptoms.\n\n**Path 2 — Surgical decompression**: Removes the bone and tissue compressing your nerves. Research shows that surgery provides greater improvement in pain and walking ability compared to conservative care in the first 2-4 years, but conservative care also improves over time. Surgery has risks including infection, nerve injury, and the need for repeat surgery.\'\n\n**Step 2 — Use Decision Aids**:\n- Validated decision aids for lumbar stenosis exist (e.g., Ottawa Decision Guide adapted for spinal stenosis)\n- Present outcome probabilities: approximately 60-70% of surgical patients report meaningful improvement at 2 years vs 30-45% of conservatively managed patients\n- Discuss that improvement with conservative care is slower but real, and some patients deteriorate with either approach\n\n**Step 3 — Explore the Patient\'s Values**:\n\'What matters most to you? Is it getting back to longer walks as quickly as possible? Is it avoiding surgery altogether? Is it minimizing risk? Your answer helps us figure out which path is right for you.\'\n\n**Step 4 — Address the Blood Transfusion Issue Directly and Respectfully**:\n\n\'I want to address your faith conviction about blood transfusions because it is important to your surgical planning and I want to make sure we handle it correctly.\n\nThe good news is that lumbar decompression surgery for stenosis is not a high blood-loss procedure. Typical blood loss for a standard laminectomy is 100-300 mL, and blood transfusion is rarely needed. However, there is always a small risk of unexpected bleeding.\n\nIf you decide to pursue surgery, here is what we would do to respect your wishes and maximize your safety:\n\n1. **Bloodless surgery planning**: Refer to a surgeon experienced with Jehovah\'s Witness patients or a hospital with a bloodless surgery program. Many major medical centers have dedicated programs.\n2. **Preoperative optimization**: Check hemoglobin and iron stores. If anemic, treat with IV iron and/or erythropoietin (EPO) before surgery to build up your blood count.\n3. **Intraoperative techniques**: Cell saver (some Jehovah\'s Witnesses accept this because the blood remains in a continuous circuit — this is an individual decision of conscience), meticulous hemostasis, tranexamic acid to reduce surgical bleeding, controlled hypotension.\n4. **Advance directive**: A detailed surgical consent and advance directive specifying exactly which blood products you will and will not accept. Some Jehovah\'s Witnesses accept certain blood fractions (albumin, clotting factors, immunoglobulins) — this is a personal decision. We would have this conversation in detail before surgery.\n5. **Anesthesia consultation**: Preoperative anesthesia planning specifically addressing the no-transfusion protocol.\'\n\n**Step 5 — Document the Decision-Making Process**:\n- Document that the patient was informed of all options, risks, and benefits\n- Document his religious beliefs and the specific blood product restrictions\n- Ensure the surgical advance directive is completed, signed, and accessible in the medical record and the operating room\n- Include a witnessed conversation that the patient understands the risk of refusing transfusion in the rare event of significant hemorrhage\n\n**Step 6 — Respect the Decision**:\nRegardless of which path the patient chooses — continued conservative care, surgery with a bloodless protocol, or a hybrid approach — the physician\'s role is to provide information, ensure understanding, respect autonomy, and support the decision. The patient\'s religious conviction is not a barrier to be overcome but a value to be incorporated into the treatment plan.',
