    const Section(
      title: '+ Address the anesthesiologist\'s question about overriding the patient\'s blood transfusion refusal, and demonstrate how you would facilitate the preoperative conversation.',
      content: '**Responding to the Anesthesiologist**:\n\n\'I understand your concern — you want to protect the patient\'s life, and that instinct is what makes you a good physician. But the answer is no, we should not seek a court order, and I want to explain why.\n\nThis patient is a competent adult who has made an informed decision based on a deeply held religious belief. The legal and ethical framework is clear:\n\n1. **Patient autonomy**: A competent adult has the legal right to refuse any medical treatment, including life-saving treatment. This is protected by the First Amendment, the Patient Self-Determination Act, and decades of case law.\n2. **Informed refusal**: He has been informed of the risks of refusing blood transfusion, including the possibility of death from hemorrhage. He understands and accepts this risk. His decision is documented.\n3. **Court orders**: Courts have consistently upheld the right of competent adult Jehovah\'s Witness patients to refuse blood transfusion, even when refusal may result in death. Seeking a court order against a competent patient\'s clearly expressed wishes would be ethically inappropriate and legally unlikely to succeed.\n4. **The distinction that matters**: Court orders are sometimes sought for minors or for patients who lack decision-making capacity. Neither applies here.\n\nOur obligation is to provide the best possible surgical care within his stated boundaries — not to override those boundaries.\'\n\n**Facilitating the Preoperative Conversation with the Patient**:\n\nI would bring together the patient, his wife, the surgeon, and the anesthesiologist for a structured preoperative conference:\n\n\'[Patient name], we are meeting today to make sure everyone on your surgical team understands your wishes and that we have a clear plan that respects your beliefs while giving you the safest surgery possible.\n\nI want to walk through this together so there is no confusion on the day of surgery.\'\n\n**Step 1 — Clarify specific boundaries**:\n\'Every Jehovah\'s Witness patient I have worked with has slightly different personal decisions about what they will and will not accept. I want to make sure we understand yours specifically.\'\n\nReview a detailed blood product consent form:\n- **Whole blood**: Refused (universal among Jehovah\'s Witnesses)\n- **Packed red blood cells**: Refused\n- **Plasma**: Refused\n- **Platelets**: Refused\n- **Albumin**: Conscience decision — some accept, some decline\n- **Clotting factor concentrates**: Conscience decision\n- **Immunoglobulins**: Conscience decision\n- **Erythropoietin**: Generally accepted (not a blood product)\n- **Cell saver with continuous circuit**: Conscience decision — many accept this\n- **Hemodilution (pre-surgical blood draw kept in continuous circuit)**: Conscience decision\n\n**Step 2 — Confirm understanding of risks**:\n\'I want to make sure you understand that while the risk of significant bleeding during this surgery is low, if unexpected hemorrhage occurs and your hemoglobin drops to a critically low level, we would not be able to give you blood products per your wishes. In an extreme scenario, this could be life-threatening. Do you understand and accept this risk?\'\n\n**Step 3 — Confirm consistency under pressure**:\n\'I also want to ask — and this is important — if you are in the operating room and the situation becomes serious, your wishes as documented today will be followed. We will not ask your wife to override your decision while you are under anesthesia. Is that consistent with what you want?\'\n\n**Step 4 — Document thoroughly**:\n- Detailed advance directive specifying each blood product category\n- Signed by patient, witnessed by two staff members\n- Copy to surgeon, anesthesiologist, OR nursing, medical record\n- Hospital Jehovah\'s Witness liaison (many hospitals have one) should be notified\n\n**Step 5 — Address the wife**:\n\'Mrs. [name], I want to make sure you are also comfortable with this plan and that you understand your husband\'s wishes. If a situation arises during surgery where a decision needs to be made, we will follow his advance directive. Is there anything you want to discuss?\'\n\n**Key principles**: Respecting autonomy, informed refusal, team communication, thorough documentation, involving family, and providing the best possible care within the patient\'s stated boundaries.',
